<h2>Configuration modules and Elisp packages</h2>

<blockquote>
  <p>What is the motivation to divide between what seems to be like e.g.
prot-lisp consisting of what seems to be just functions (library)
and prot-emacs-modules which seems to be just package configuration
(like what you’d fine in the body of a use-package declaration)?</p>
</blockquote>

<p>I keep <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">prot-emacs-modules</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">prot-lisp</code> separate because they have
a different role. The latter consists of what effectively are Emacs
Lisp “packages”. I write them in the same style as actual packages
that I maintain (check <a href="https://protesilaos.com/emacs">https://protesilaos.com/emacs</a>).</p>

<p>The modules are the specifics of my configuration. They have the exact
preferences I rely on in my current session. A package may define
options. The module picks one among them.</p>

<p>This is exactly what we all do when we configure a package from GNU
ELPA, for example: the package defines options and in our <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">init.el</code> we
set it up as we see fit.</p>

<p>Strictly speaking, this distinction is not necessary for a personal
Emacs configuration. All the code is mine. I could easily hardcode
values, not provide abstractions, and not bother with clear points of
entry. Doing so would work well for my use-case, which is the main
goal of all this code, anyway. But that would be a counterproductive
habit; “counterproductive” because I want the code I write to be easy
for others to copy and adapt. It also conditions me to be more
thoughtful with my programs and more organised with my writings.</p>

<p>There are some exceptions where a function is defined in the modules.
This is either because the function really is about my configuration
or it is part of some experiment I am doing.</p>

