<h2>About not using <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">use-package</code></h2>

<blockquote>
  <p>It seems you don’t use use-package (I see <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">(require ...</code> in the
modules) anymore? Curious why.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>I consider this “sand in the gears”. It forces those who read my
configuration to slow down and be careful about what they are copying.
If they just paste my Elisp into their setup, they will get an error.
Slowing down gives them the chance to read the code and think about
what they are working towards.</p>

<p>Yes, this sounds like I am doing them a disservice. But I think they
will benefit from this practice long-term. Copying willy-nilly is how
you never learn.</p>

<p>Concretely about the use of <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">require</code>, in the modules I use my own
macros. Every <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">require</code> should be wrapped in a <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">prot-emacs-configure</code>.
Not all my modules rely on <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">require</code> though. I have some packages that
are loaded when a certain condition is met, typically via a hook.</p>

<p>In <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">prot-lisp/</code> the use of <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">require</code> is standard: this is how packages
are supposed to do it.</p>

<p>By the way, writing my own configuration macros has helped me
understand things at a deeper level. It also gives me maximum control
over my setup. But the real reason is the sand in the gears that I
alluded to.</p>

<h2>Autoloads and deferred loading</h2>

<blockquote>
  <p>Is there any reason to autoload features? I’ve come across some
guides on using Emacs and it seems people tend to prefer to autoload
everything as much as possible but I feel like it only makes sense
to autoload only features you might not use during a session. The
time to load on startup when you front load instead of autoload
seems less important than the time it takes to load when you
actually use a feature during a session. Especially if running Emacs
as a server or using a single long-running instance.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>With <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">use-package</code> you get deferred loading when you use keywords such
as <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">:bind</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">:hook</code>. Internally, this depends on the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">autoload</code>
mechanism that Emacs has. Autoloads are hints that inform Emacs about
the existence of some function or variable in a certain file. When
that symbol is called for the first time before its file is loaded,
Emacs goes to the corresponding file, evaluates everything, and then
runs the original code. For example:</p>

<div class="language-elisp highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">autoload</span> <span class="ss">'prot-test-hello</span> <span class="s">"prot-test"</span><span class="p">)</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>If we call <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">prot-test-hello</code>, Emacs evaluates <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">prot-test.el</code>, and runs
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">prot-test-hello</code>. This works only if the file is in the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">load-path</code>.</p>

<p>Users generally do not need to understand the technicalities. When
installing a package, Emacs generates an index of all the autoloads
automatically. If you are curious, go to the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">elpa/</code> subdirectory of
your Emacs directory, find a package, and read the autolaods file
(e.g. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">denote-autoloads.el</code>).</p>

<p>Strictly speaking, deferred loading is not necessary. If you are going
run Emacs for several hours non-stop, then the ~60 seconds it takes to
load an average configuration do not matter. Even ten minutes are not
be important in such a scenario: you can brew a coffee in the meantime.</p>

<p>Deferred loading is still beneficial though:</p>

<ul>
  <li>It makes you think about the relationship between the various parts
of your configuration. Having an Emacs that loads fast and works
well means that you have understood how you are piecing things
together.</li>
  <li>Deferred loading makes it easier to experiment with your
configuration and to restart Emacs when necessary. Imagine wanting
to try things out, restart Emacs, and wait for several minutes each
time… That breaks your flow.</li>
</ul>

<p>Autoloads help you discover new features. For example, when you press
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-x</code> in a generic Emacs you are presented with thousands of options.
Most of those are not loaded yet. Emacs just knows the symbols and
will load their files when you make the selection. A package developer
can leverage this feature to provide functionality that may be useful
only under certain circumstances. That is a helpful way for the user
to notice the before and after states. It also gives the user maximum
control of what to load and when, which improves confidence in one’s
Emacs configuration.</p>

<h2>GUI or terminal</h2>

<blockquote>
  <p>Do you ever run Emacs on the terminal or GUI only and do you always
have a single long-running instance or do you sometimes use multiple
instances?</p>
</blockquote>

<p>I always use the GUI. It can combine different font families and font
sizes and it can also display images—how else am I supposed to check
my selfies? The terminal is fine in its own right and is especially
useful for remote connections. But I do not have that use-case.</p>

<p>My terminal emulator is <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">xterm</code>, by the way. It works well, though I
do not rely on it. Everything that could be done in a terminal is
handled by Emacs. I also am on Debian stable, so trying the newest
programs is neither a straightforward option nor a priority: I have a
stable setup and focus on getting work done.</p>

<p>I use a single instance of Emacs. In my configuration I also have
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">server-start</code>. This gives me the option to evaluate Emacs Lisp code
even when Emacs is not focused, such as when I switch the theme of my
entire desktop environment or window manager.</p>

<p>I do not run Emacs for long hours. When I am done with whatever I am
working on, I switch off the computer. The typical workflow is for 2-3
hours maximum. Then I will do some outdoor activity.</p>

<h2>Choice of keybindings and Evil</h2>

<blockquote>
  <p>Any thoughts on using Evil?</p>
</blockquote>

<p>It is fine and I am indifferent to it. What makes Emacs so nice is
that you can evaluate Emacs Lisp live and work with the new state. The
choice of keybindings is a personal choice. There is no better or
worse.</p>

<p>People will, of course, argue about which paradigm is more efficient.
This is the kind of knowledge that seldom translates into real-world
productivity. The bottleneck is how fast and how well you can think.
Then what matters are the qualities of your output. Moving text around
quickly does not make you productive per se or, indeed, a decent
writer/programmer.</p>

<p>This is a matter of comfort though, which can indirectly influence
your productivity simply by improving your mood and willingness to
write. I put colours and fonts in that same category. If it makes you
feel better, then use it.</p>

<p>That granted, to configure <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">evil-mode</code> properly you need to have a
solid understanding of Emacs and be willing to control every part of
your setup. Otherwise you will run into cases where something does not
work as expected and you will not know how exactly to change it.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>It seems like you’re willing to embrace Emacs defaults–is there
anything you’ve still maintained Emacs to be more Vim-like, e.g.
modal and arguably more ergnomic/mnemonic?</p>
</blockquote>

<p>The general scheme of Emacs default keybindings is good enough for me.
Though I change plenty of keybindings to do what I want. Actually, I
plan to rebind basics such as <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">C-n</code>, but I have not found the time to
do it thoroughly. There are many default keybindings that I do not
like.</p>

<p>I do not think Vim is ergonomic by default. Impulsively reaching out
to the Escape key is not helpful, especially if you are twisting the
wrist in the process. Typing Shift with the relatively weak small
fingers is not beneficial either. Yes, you can circumvent those, but I
am referring to the out-of-the-box experience, because if you are
thinking about making changes then you might as well do the same for
the Emacs-style keys.</p>

<p>Emacs keys are not ergonomic either. Ergonomics is a matter of the
entire office setup, from the keyboard, to the desk, the placement of
the monitor, the typing style. I have an article on the matter:
<a href="https://protesilaos.com/codelog/2026-05-04-emacs-keyboard-ergonomics/">https://protesilaos.com/codelog/2026-05-04-emacs-keyboard-ergonomics/</a>.</p>

<p>My custom keyboard has <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">hjkl</code> mapped to the arrow keys on a so-called
“layer” that I can activate with my thumbs. The <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">yuio</code> keys above
correspond to <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Home</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">PageDn</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">PageUp</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">End</code>, respectively. Those
are the bigger motion counterparts of the cardinal directions. Extend
this to <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Ctrl</code> and one of those keys to perform a different motion,
like <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">C-&lt;home&gt;</code> to go to the beginning of the buffer, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">C-&lt;right&gt;</code> to
move forward by a word. This works everywhere, by the way.</p>

<p>I actually have plenty of articles on the topic of keyboards, if you
are so inclined: <a href="https://protesilaos.com/keeb">https://protesilaos.com/keeb</a>.</p>

<h2>Persisting Emacs sessions</h2>

<blockquote>
  <p>I may have missed this, but how do you approach persistent session
(if at all)?</p>
</blockquote>

<p>I do not. This is not necessary in my workflow. I have bookmarks for
the buffers/files I visit frequently. Then I have Git projects that I
always keep in a clean state with a history that is meaningful. When I
need to work on something, I switch to the bookmark/project and start
from where I left it.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>I like that if I return a project I’ve worked on a few weeks ago,
restoring these aspects is a good way to suggest where I last left
off instead of starting from scratch (little things like window
layout or set of open buffers is suggestive of progress of the
project).</p>
</blockquote>

<p>This is a matter of how one’s brain works. Mine is more abstract, so
having things in a specific spatial arrangement does not add much
value. Configuring Emacs to take care of all relevant cases is thus
not appealing to me. I will commit my changes and leave everything in
a condition that makes the next step obvious. I am happy to write
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">TODO</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">FIXME</code> comments in my code and/or have a relevant task in
my Org agenda. This is a matter of logical sequencing. It is easier
for me to reason about it.</p>

<p>I used to load the built-in <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">desktop.el</code>. Then I realised that loading
hundreds of buffers for something that I might not need is
counter-productive. Furthermore, storing a specific state for a
potential future occasion requires that I remember to load the right
file, manage all such files, and so on, which is cognitively
burdensome and ultimately no more efficient than working with Git.</p>
